<div class="post"><h1>A small café that refuses to grow</h1><p class="meta">by Ana Lima</p>
<p>The café on Mill Street has eight tables, one coffee machine and a menu that has not changed since it opened in 1998. Twice a year, someone offers to buy it and turn it into a chain. Twice a year, the owner says no.</p>
<p>“People come here because it is the same every day,” she told me while she cleaned the counter. “If I open ten more, it will not be this place any more. It will be a brand.”</p>
<p>Her regular customers agree. A retired teacher has ordered the same tea and the same lemon cake every afternoon for eleven years, and he sits at the same table by the window.</p>
<p>The café makes enough money to pay its three workers well and to close every August for a long holiday. For the owner, that is success. She does not want more, and she does not see why she should.</p></div>

